## Development

Run any of these from the monorepo root. Replace `npm install` with
`yarn install` / `pnpm install` if that's your preferred package manager —
the workspace works the same either way.

```bash
# Install dependencies
npm install

# Lint
npx nx lint <%= name %>

# Type-check
npx nx run <%= name %>:check-types

# Test
npx nx test <%= name %>

# Deploy
npx nx run <%= name %>:deploy
```

## Structure

<%_ const usesCommerceLib = hasAdminUI || hasBusinessConfig || hasCommerceWebhooks; _%>
<%_ if (usesCommerceLib) { _%>
This app integrates with Adobe Commerce via App Management
(`@adobe/aio-commerce-lib-app`). The merchant-facing config, eventing, webhook
subscriptions, and custom install steps are all declared in
`app.commerce.config.ts`; the lib provisions them at install time.

Everything under `src/*/.generated/` is written by the lib during
`pre-app-build` and is gitignored — treat it as disposable. That includes the
target of the `#app.commerce.config` import alias, which the lib also adds to
`package.json`.

If you import `#app.commerce.config` from your own action code, add an ambient
declaration under `global-types/` for the fields you read. The alias resolves to
a gitignored file, so on a fresh clone (and in CI before a build) TypeScript
would otherwise fail with `Cannot find module` — the `package.json` entry alone
doesn't help, because the file it points at isn't there yet:

```ts
// global-types/app-commerce-config.d.ts — no top-level import/export, so the
// `declare module` below stays global.
declare module '#app.commerce.config' {
    const config: { metadata: { id: string; displayName: string } };
    export default config;
}
```
<%_ if (hasAdminUI) { _%>

The admin UI lives under `src/commerce-backend-ui-2/` (the
`commerce/backend-ui/2` extension point). Its menu item is declared under
`adminUi` in `app.commerce.config.ts`; the lib regenerates
`src/commerce-backend-ui-2/ext.config.yaml` from that on every build, so don't
edit it by hand.

Add pages under `web-src/src/pages/` and wire them up in `web-src/src/app.tsx`.
`createExtensionApp()` already handles registration with the Commerce Admin
host, the shared context, routing and the Spectrum provider — use `useIms()`
for IMS credentials rather than reaching for the guest connection.

To call a backend action from the UI, add its `<package>/<action>` key to
`web-src/src/types/ActionName.ts` and use `useAppBuilderAction` /
`useLazyAppBuilderAction`. URLs come from `action-urls.generated.json`, which
`scripts/generate-action-registry.mjs` writes during `pre-app-build` — select a
workspace with `aio app use` first or that step fails.
<%_ } _%>
<%_ } else { _%>
This is a standalone Adobe App Builder integration. Backend actions live under
`src/actions/`. There is no Commerce admin UI and no business configuration.
<%_ } _%>